-        "note": "THE RANGE IS BRACKETED BY ITS TWO WITNESSES, AND BOTH ARE PRE-1835. IT OPENS 24 FEBRUARY 1830, the date on the Harrison plan, because that is the earliest date any source this project holds fixes to this building; Hubbard's narrative of the fort's surroundings is of the late 1820s and carries it earlier without dating it. IT CLOSES 29 DECEMBER 1836, the evacuation, and that is a floor rather than a demolition: nothing attests when the fort's outer buildings came down, and a range that ran on to 1856 with the fort's principal structures would claim a survival for a barn that nobody wrote down. WHAT CARRIES THE SCENE DATE OF 1 JULY 1835 is the middle of that range and not either end of it — the post was garrisoned continuously from June 1832 to 29 December 1836, so a service building the garrison's own engineer drew in 1830 is standing five years later unless something says it stopped, and nothing does. That is an argument, not an assumption, and it is the argument every Fort Dearborn record here rests on.",
+        "note": "THE RANGE IS BRACKETED BY ITS TWO WITNESSES, AND BOTH ARE PRE-1835. IT OPENS 24 FEBRUARY 1830, the date on the Harrison plan, because that is the earliest date any source this project holds fixes to this building; Hubbard's narrative of the fort's surroundings is of the late 1820s and carries it earlier without dating it. IT CLOSES 29 DECEMBER 1836, the evacuation, and that is a floor rather than a demolition: nothing attests when the fort's outer buildings came down, and a range that ran on to 1856 with the fort's principal structures would claim a survival for a barn that nobody wrote down. WHAT CARRIES THE SCENE DATE OF 1 JULY 1835 is the middle of that range and not either end of it \u2014 the post was garrisoned continuously from June 1832 to 29 December 1836, so a service building the garrison's own engineer drew in 1830 is standing five years later unless something says it stopped, and nothing does. That is an argument, not an assumption, and it is the argument every Fort Dearborn record here rests on.",
