-        "note": "TWO WITNESSES PUT THIS BARN IN THE SAME PLACE, TWICE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS INFERRED RATHER THAN CONJECTURAL. The plate names it — Big Barn with Cupola, lettered beside a drawn building west-south-west of the fort — and Gurdon Hubbard, describing the same ground from memory of the late 1820s, writes: 'West, and a little south, of the fort was the military barn, adjoining which, on the east, was the fort garden, of about four acres' (hubbard_autobiography_1911, leaf 75, printed p. 37, committed text lines 2392-2399). THE SECOND CLAUSE IS THE CHECK, and it is a strong one: the fort garden is already committed, independently, as fort_dearborn_garrison_garden. Its polygon's nearest corner stands 36.4 m from the position this record derives, and the garden lies east and a little south of the barn — which is Hubbard's sentence, in coordinates neither he nor this record's derivation had sight of. Nothing was moved to make that come out.\n\nREAD OFF THE 1830 HARRISON PLAN, BY THE SAME RULE AS THE ELEVEN RECORDS ALREADY TAKEN FROM IT. Internet Archive leaf n242 of historyofchicago01andr (Andreas vol. 1, p. 113), rotated 90 degrees anticlockwise so the sheet's own true-north arrow stands vertical, is 2715 x 2003 px. On that image the stockade's ink runs from x 1356 to x 1535 and from y 553 to y 730, so the enclosure's centre is pixel (1445, 644); the committed palisade record hangs that centre on local E +1152, N +221, which is UTM 448224.70 E, 4637616.80 N. Pixels convert at 1.10 ft/px (0.33528 m/px), the scale docs/RESEARCH/fort_dearborn.md section 3 derives from the commandant's quarters. THE ANCHOR AND THE SCALE ARE CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED, and the check is the garrison garden — the one thing on this plate that is both drawn and already committed. Its committed centre lands at pixel (1209, 1008) under this transform; the drawn diamond's centre measures (1207, 1002). Seven pixels, 2.3 m. A second check runs the other way: the committed blockhouse centre predicts pixel (1385, 698) and the drawn blockhouse square measures (1375, 710), 16 px or 5.4 m. WHAT THE TRANSFORM CANNOT DO is beat its own inputs. The enclosure measures 180 px across at this scale, which is 60 m against the committed palisade's 53 m — a 13 % spread, inside the +/-20 % the scale carries, and the reason is visible on the plate: 180 px is the OUTER edge of an engraved line four to six pixels thick. So carry +/-20 % on this offset from the fort centre and +/-20 m on the fort itself, exactly as every plate-derived record here does.\n\nTHE PIXEL. The drawn barn is a hatched parallelogram whose four corners measure (1118, 872), (1140, 892), (1110, 925) and (1088, 905), centre (1114.0, 898.5) — 331 px west and 254.5 px south of the fort centre, which is 111.0 m west and 85.3 m south of it.\n\nIT IS NOT BEAUBIEN'S BARN, and the question is worth answering because the two stand 50 m apart, which is inside the error either carries. data/structures/beaubien_barn.json is the old cabin Jean Baptiste Beaubien had lived in before 1817 and afterwards used as a barn, placed by eye inside his own group on Block 5; this is the garrison's barn, drawn on a military plan inside the fort's fenced ground and called 'the military barn' by a man who walked through it. Different owner, different evidence, different enclosure. They are not distinguishable by distance and they do not need to be.",
+        "note": "TWO WITNESSES PUT THIS BARN IN THE SAME PLACE, TWICE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS INFERRED RATHER THAN CONJECTURAL. The plate names it \u2014 Big Barn with Cupola, lettered beside a drawn building west-south-west of the fort \u2014 and Gurdon Hubbard, describing the same ground from memory of the late 1820s, writes: 'West, and a little south, of the fort was the military barn, adjoining which, on the east, was the fort garden, of about four acres' (hubbard_autobiography_1911, leaf 75, printed p. 37, committed text lines 2392-2399). THE SECOND CLAUSE IS THE CHECK, and it is a strong one: the fort garden is already committed, independently, as fort_dearborn_garrison_garden. Its polygon's nearest corner stands 36.4 m from the position this record derives, and the garden lies east and a little south of the barn \u2014 which is Hubbard's sentence, in coordinates neither he nor this record's derivation had sight of. Nothing was moved to make that come out.\n\nREAD OFF THE 1830 HARRISON PLAN, BY THE SAME RULE AS THE ELEVEN RECORDS ALREADY TAKEN FROM IT. Internet Archive leaf n242 of historyofchicago01andr (Andreas vol. 1, p. 113), rotated 90 degrees anticlockwise so the sheet's own true-north arrow stands vertical, is 2715 x 2003 px. On that image the stockade's ink runs from x 1356 to x 1535 and from y 553 to y 730, so the enclosure's centre is pixel (1445, 644); the committed palisade record hangs that centre on local E +1152, N +221, which is UTM 448224.70 E, 4637616.80 N. Pixels convert at 1.10 ft/px (0.33528 m/px), the scale docs/RESEARCH/fort_dearborn.md section 3 derives from the commandant's quarters. THE ANCHOR AND THE SCALE ARE CHECKED, NOT ASSUMED, and the check is the garrison garden \u2014 the one thing on this plate that is both drawn and already committed. Its committed centre lands at pixel (1209, 1008) under this transform; the drawn diamond's centre measures (1207, 1002). Seven pixels, 2.3 m. A second check runs the other way: the committed blockhouse centre predicts pixel (1385, 698) and the drawn blockhouse square measures (1375, 710), 16 px or 5.4 m. WHAT THE TRANSFORM CANNOT DO is beat its own inputs. The enclosure measures 180 px across at this scale, which is 60 m against the committed palisade's 53 m \u2014 a 13 % spread, inside the +/-20 % the scale carries, and the reason is visible on the plate: 180 px is the OUTER edge of an engraved line four to six pixels thick. So carry +/-20 % on this offset from the fort centre and +/-20 m on the fort itself, exactly as every plate-derived record here does.\n\nTHE PIXEL. The drawn barn is a hatched parallelogram whose four corners measure (1118, 872), (1140, 892), (1110, 925) and (1088, 905), centre (1114.0, 898.5) \u2014 331 px west and 254.5 px south of the fort centre, which is 111.0 m west and 85.3 m south of it.\n\nIT IS NOT BEAUBIEN'S BARN, and the question is worth answering because the two stand 50 m apart, which is inside the error either carries. data/structures/beaubien_barn.json is the old cabin Jean Baptiste Beaubien had lived in before 1817 and afterwards used as a barn, placed by eye inside his own group on Block 5; this is the garrison's barn, drawn on a military plan inside the fort's fenced ground and called 'the military barn' by a man who walked through it. Different owner, different evidence, different enclosure. They are not distinguishable by distance and they do not need to be.",
